          },
          "metadata": {}
        }
      ],
      "source": [
        "# Apply random noise on clean images\n",
        "noisy_imgs = np.random.normal(data_digit.data, 5)\n",
        "# Display the noisy images\n",
        "display_digits(noisy_imgs)"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "id": "3092bb8b",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "3092bb8b"
      },
      "source": [
        "## Question 3.4 Does PCA mitigate the random noise?\n",
        "\n",
        "- Perform feature reconstruction on <code>noisy\\_imgs</code> (preserving $80\\%$ variance after applying PCA for the dimension reduction)\n",
        "\n",
        "- Explain your understandings on why PCA reconstructs cleaner images."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": 22,
      "id": "f55abf3b",
      "metadata": {
        "execution": {
          "iopub.execute_input": "2026-10-22T05:16:44.523021Z",
          "iopub.status.busy": "2026-10-22T05:16:44.522865Z",
          "iopub.status.idle": "2026-10-22T05:16:45.023216Z",
          "shell.execute_reply": "2026-10-22T05:16:45.022802Z"
        },
        "colab": {
          "base_uri": "https://localhost:8080/",
          "height": 423
        },
        "id": "f55abf3b",
        "outputId": "19641988-08db-4572-b5cb-047fc15c73ff"
      },
      "outputs": [
        {
          "output_type": "stream",
          "name": "stdout",
          "text": [
            "Number of components used: 40\n"
          ]
        },
        {
          "output_type": "display_data",
          "data": {
            "text/plain": [
              "<Figure size 640x480 with 15 Axes>"
            ],
            "image/png": "\n"
          },
          "metadata": {}
        }
      ],
      "source": [
        "\n",
        "# Fit PCA on the noisy images, keeping enough components to preserve 80% variance.\n",
        "pca_noise = PCA(n_components=0.8)\n",
        "lower_dimension_noise = pca_noise.fit_transform(noisy_imgs)\n",
        "reconstructed_noise = pca_noise.inverse_transform(lower_dimension_noise)\n",
        "\n",
        "print(\"Number of components used:\", pca_noise.n_components_)\n",
        "# Display the reconstructed images\n",
        "display_digits(reconstructed_noise)\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "id": "74d38b71",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "74d38b71"
      },
      "source": [
        "### Your written answers for Question 3.4:\n",
        "\n",
        "PCA helps clean up random noise because it compresses each image to its core component focusing on  the big pattern which is shared across the dataset. When the images are reconstructed, only the top components are used and the small and irregular variations in it get left behind in turn making the digits look sharper. But it can't catch every bit of noise and some genuine details in the image can be lost along the way.\n",
        "\n",
        "\n"
      ]
    }
  ],
